    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Constitutional Basis of Parliamentary Control\nUnder the Constitution of India, ultimate financial power resides with Parliament:\n- **Article 265:** No tax shall be levied or collected except by authority of law.\n- **Article 266:** Consolidated Fund of India and Public Account. No money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund without an Appropriation Act passed by Parliament (Article 266(3)).\n- **Article 112:** Annual Financial Statement (Budget) presented by the President to Parliament.\n- **Article 114:** Appropriation Bills granting legal authority to incur expenditure.\n- **Article 115:** Supplementary, additional, or excess grants.\n- **Article 151:** Audit reports of the Comptroller and Auditor General (C&AG) laid before each House of Parliament.\n\n### 2. Canons of Financial Propriety (Rule 116, IRFC Vol. I)\nEvery railway officer exercising financial powers must observe four cardinal principles:\n1. **Prudence & Vigilance (The Prudent Man Rule):** The expenditure should not be prima facie more than the occasion demands. Every railway servant must exercise the same vigilance and care in respect of expenditure incurred from public moneys as a person of ordinary prudence would exercise in respect of expenditure of his own money.\n2. **Sanction & Authority:** The expenditure should not be sanctioned by an authority which will accrue directly or indirectly to its own advantage.\n3. **No Personal Benefit / Favors:** Public money should not be utilized for the benefit of a particular person or section of the community unless:\n   - The amount of expenditure involved is insignificant; or\n   - A claim for the amount could be enforced in a Court of Law; or\n   - The expenditure is in pursuance of a recognized policy or custom.\n4. **Appropriation Bounds:** The amount of allowances granted to meet expenditure of a particular type should be so regulated that the allowances are not on the whole a source of profit to the recipients (e.g., Travelling Allowance, Daily Allowance).\n\n### 3. Public Accountability & Parliamentary Committees\n- **Public Accounts Committee (PAC):**\n  - Consists of 22 members (15 Lok Sabha, 7 Rajya Sabha), chaired by an Opposition member.\n  - Examines Appropriation Accounts and the Reports of the C&AG on Railways.\n  - Scrutinizes financial irregularities, cost overruns, wasteful expenditure, and infructuous contracts.\n- **Estimates Committee:** Examines whether funds are properly laid out within the limits of policy and suggests alternative organizational efficiencies.\n- **Committee on Public Undertakings (COPU):** Examines railway PSUs (CRIS, IRCTC, IRCON, RITES, RVNL, DFCCIL, CONCOR).\n",
    "deprecated_vs_modern": {
      "deprecated_practice": "Separate presentation of the Railway Budget before the General Budget (Convention dating back to Acworth Committee 1920-21).",
      "modern_practice": "Merger of Railway Budget with the Union Budget from financial year 2017-18; single Appropriation Bill.",
      "transition_notes": "Even after merger, parliamentary control over railway expenditure remains through Demand for Grants No. 85 (Ministry of Railways) and detailed Pink Book scrutiny."
    }
