    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Transforming Capital Funding: GBS vs EBR (IRFC)\nIn recent years, Indian Railways has witnessed record capital expenditure (Capex exceeding Rs. 2.5 Lakh Crore annually):\n- **Gross Budgetary Support (GBS):** Capital funds provided directly from the General Budget by the Central Government.\n- **Extra Budgetary Resources (EBR):**\n  - Raised through the **Indian Railway Finance Corporation (IRFC)** via market bonds, institutional borrowing (e.g., LIC loan facility), and external multilateral funding (World Bank, JICA, ADB).\n  - IRFC borrows funds and leases rolling stock (locos, coaches, wagons) to Indian Railways under long-term lease agreements.\n\n### 2. PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan & National Rail Plan 2030\n- **PM Gati Shakti:**\n  - A GIS-based digital platform bringing 16 ministries (Railways, Roadways, Ports, Aviation) together for synchronized project execution.\n  - Creation of **Gati Shakti Units (GSUs)** in all railway divisions headed by a Chief Project Manager (CPM) to fast-track survey, DPR, tendering, and execution of multimodal cargo terminals (GCTs).\n- **National Rail Plan (NRP) 2030:**\n  - Aims to create capacity ahead of demand.\n  - Objective: Increase railway share in national freight movement from **27% to 45% by 2030**.\n  - Commissioning of Western & Eastern Dedicated Freight Corridors (DFC), high-speed rail corridors, and 100% route electrification.\n\n### 3. Treasury Single Account (TSA) System\n- Financial reform spearheaded by Ministry of Finance and RBI.\n- Autonomous bodies and agencies implementing railway projects receive funds on a just-in-time basis through electronic authorization, preventing parking of public funds in commercial bank accounts and saving interest costs for the government.\n",
