    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. The Pyramid of Information Systems in Railway EDP\n```\n                     \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                     \u2502  Executive Support      \u2502  Strategic Level\n                     \u2502  Systems (ESS / EIS)    \u2502  (Railway Board / GM)\n                     \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524  Trend analysis, Capex decisions\n                     \u2502  Decision Support       \u2502  Tactical Level\n                     \u2502  Systems (DSS)          \u2502  (PCOM, PCCM, PFA)\n                     \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524  Route rationalization, tariff models\n                     \u2502  Management Info        \u2502  Management Level\n                     \u2502  Systems (MIS)          \u2502  (DRM, Sr.DOM, Sr.DCM)\n                     \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524  Punctuality reports, fuel consumption\n                     \u2502  Transaction Processing \u2502  Operational Level\n                     \u2502  Systems (TPS)          \u2502  (Booking Clerks, Controllers, Sheds)\n                     \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518  PRS, UTS, FOIS, IPAS, UDM\n```\n\n### 2. TPS vs MIS vs DSS: Deep Comparison\n| Feature | Transaction Processing System (TPS) | Management Information System (MIS) | Decision Support System (DSS) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Users** | Frontline operators, booking clerks | Middle managers, Branch officers | Senior leadership, planners |\n| **Focus** | Daily operational tracking, data entry | Periodic summary, variance reports | Ad-hoc queries, predictive models |\n| **Data Nature** | Highly detailed, current, transactional | Aggregated, structured, internal | Multidimensional, historical + external |\n| **Processing** | Real-time OLTP, high concurrency | Scheduled batch generation | Interactive OLAP, simulation, what-if |\n| **Railway Example** | **PRS** ticket issue, **UTS** suburban booking | Daily train punctuality MIS, fuel log summary | Dynamic pricing optimizer, rake routing optimizer |\n\n### 3. Data Storage Fundamentals in DBMS\n- **Data Hierarchy:** Bit -> Byte (Character) -> Field (Column) -> Record (Row / Tuple) -> File (Table) -> Database.\n- **Physical Page / Block:** Smallest physical unit of I/O transfer between disk and main memory (typically 4KB to 8KB in Oracle / SQL Server / PostgreSQL).\n- **Buffer Cache / Buffer Pool:** Shared memory area in RAM holding recently read data blocks to minimize disk I/O.\n- **Row-Store vs Column-Store:**\n  - *Row-Store:* Efficient for OLTP (fast single-row inserts and updates).\n  - *Column-Store:* Efficient for OLAP / Data Warehouses (fast aggregation over single columns across millions of rows).\n",
