    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Algorithm Complexity Reference Matrix (Must Memorize for CBT!)\n| Algorithm | Time (Best) | Time (Average) | Time (Worst) | Space Complexity | Stable? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Linear Search** | $O(1)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(n)$ | $O(1)$ | N/A |\n| **Binary Search** | $O(1)$ | $O(\\log n)$ | $O(\\log n)$ | $O(1)$ | N/A |\n| **Bubble Sort** | $O(n)$ (optimized) | $O(n^2)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(1)$ | Yes |\n| **Insertion Sort** | $O(n)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(1)$ | Yes |\n| **Selection Sort** | $O(n^2)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(1)$ | No |\n| **Merge Sort** | $O(n \\log n)$ | $O(n \\log n)$ | $O(n \\log n)$ | $O(n)$ | Yes |\n| **Quick Sort** | $O(n \\log n)$ | $O(n \\log n)$ | $O(n^2)$ | $O(\\log n)$ | No |\n| **Heap Sort** | $O(n \\log n)$ | $O(n \\log n)$ | $O(n \\log n)$ | $O(1)$ | No |\n\n### 2. Core Data Structures & Railway Use Cases\n1. **Stack (Last-In, First-Out - LIFO):**\n   - Operations: `push()`, `pop()`, `peek()`.\n   - Use Cases: Function call stack, recursion, undo operations, parenthesis matching in SQL parsers.\n2. **Queue (First-In, First-Out - FIFO):**\n   - Operations: `enqueue()`, `dequeue()`.\n   - Use Cases: PRS Waiting List processing (First Come, First Served), print job spooling, network packet buffering.\n   - **Priority Queue:** Jobs processed based on urgency/priority (e.g., Tatkal ticket requests, emergency train dispatching).\n3. **Linked List:**\n   - Dynamic memory allocation; $O(1)$ insertion/deletion given pointer, $O(n)$ random access.\n   - Doubly Linked List: Both forward and backward traversal.\n4. **B-Tree and B+ Tree:**\n   - Self-balancing $m$-way search trees designed for disk storage.\n   - All leaf nodes at the same level; leaf nodes linked in a singly/doubly linked list for rapid sequential range scanning.\n   - **Core foundation of database indexes in Oracle, DB2, and SQL Server**.\n5. **Graph:**\n   - Consists of Vertices ($V$) and Edges ($E$).\n   - Used for modeling the entire Indian Railways network: stations as vertices, track sections as edges.\n   - **Dijkstra's Algorithm:** Finds shortest path / minimal transit time for train routing.\n",
