    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. Process Memory Layout in Operating Systems\n```\n        \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510 High Memory (0xFFFFFFFF)\n        \u2502           Kernel Space                   \u2502\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n        \u2502           User Stack                     \u2502 (Grows Downward \u25bc)\n        \u2502   (Local variables, function frames)     \u2502\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n        \u2502                  \u2502                       \u2502\n        \u2502                  \u25bc                       \u2502\n        \u2502                                          \u2502\n        \u2502                  \u25b2                       \u2502\n        \u2502                  \u2502                       \u2502\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n        \u2502           Runtime Heap                   \u2502 (Grows Upward \u25b2)\n        \u2502     (Dynamic memory: malloc/new)         \u2502\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n        \u2502      BSS (Block Started by Symbol)       \u2502 Uninitialized globals/statics (zeroed)\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n        \u2502           Data Segment                   \u2502 Initialized globals and statics\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n        \u2502           Text Segment (Code)            \u2502 Read-only executable binary instructions\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518 Low Memory (0x00000000)\n```\n\n### 2. Virtual Memory, Paging & Thrashing\n- **Virtual Memory:** Decouples logical program address space from physical RAM, allowing execution of processes larger than physical memory.\n- **Paging:**\n  - Logical memory divided into fixed-size **Pages**; physical memory divided into equal-sized **Frames** (typically 4KB).\n  - Memory Management Unit (**MMU**) translates Logical Address `(Page Number, Offset)` to Physical Address `(Frame Number, Offset)` using the **Page Table**.\n  - **TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer):** High-speed hardware cache in the CPU storing recent page-to-frame translations.\n- **Thrashing:**\n  - Occurs when a computer spends more time paging (swapping pages between RAM and disk) than executing instructions.\n  - Caused when total working set of all active processes exceeds available physical memory.\n  - Solution: Reduce degree of multiprogramming (suspend low-priority processes) or add more physical RAM.\n\n### 3. Linux / Unix Administration Essentials\n- **File Permissions (Octal):**\n  - Read ($r = 4$), Write ($w = 2$), Execute ($x = 1$).\n  - `chmod 755 script.sh` -> Owner: $rwx$ (7), Group: $r-x$ (5), Others: $r-x$ (5).\n  - `umask 022` -> Default created file permissions become $644$ ($666 - 022$), directories $755$ ($777 - 022$).\n- **Command Mastery:**\n  - `grep -rn \"ERROR\" /var/log/` -> Recursive search with line numbers.\n  - `awk -F: '{print $1, $3}' /etc/passwd` -> Extracts username and UID using colon delimiter.\n  - `sed -i 's/old/new/g' file.txt` -> Stream editor inline substitution.\n  - `top / htop, ps -ef, netstat -tulnp, lsof -i :8080, df -h, free -m, journalctl -u service`.\n\n### 4. Windows Server & Active Directory\n- **Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS):**\n  - Hierarchical identity and access management system.\n  - Hierarchy: **Forest** (top-level container) -> **Tree** -> **Domain** -> **Organizational Unit (OU)**.\n  - **Domain Controller (DC):** Server running AD DS responding to authentication requests.\n  - **Authentication:** Uses **Kerberos v5** protocol for single sign-on (SSO); LDAP for directory queries.\n  - **Group Policy Objects (GPO):** Centralized deployment of security baselines, password policies, and software across thousands of domain-joined railway client PCs.\n- **File Systems: NTFS vs FAT32:**\n  - *NTFS:* Supports file-level security ACLs, encryption (EFS), compression, journaling (fault tolerance), disk quotas, maximum file size 16 TB+.\n  - *FAT32:* Max file size **4 GB**, max partition size 32 GB / 2 TB, no file-level security or journaling.\n",
