    "detailed_notes": "\n### 1. The Virtual Machine Architecture: JVM vs CLR\n```\n     JAVA WORKFLOW                              .NET WORKFLOW\n   [Java Source Code (.java)]              [C# / VB.NET Source (.cs)]\n              \u2502                                        \u2502\n              \u25bc (javac)                                \u25bc (csc)\n    [Bytecode (.class)]                     [Common Intermediate Language (CIL)]\n              \u2502                                        \u2502\n              \u25bc                                        \u25bc\n   \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510                \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n   \u2502 JAVA VIRTUAL MACHINE  \u2502                \u2502 COMMON LANGUAGE       \u2502\n   \u2502        (JVM)          \u2502                \u2502 RUNTIME (CLR)         \u2502\n   \u2502  - ClassLoader        \u2502                \u2502  - Class Loader       \u2502\n   \u2502  - Bytecode Verifier  \u2502                \u2502  - JIT Compiler       \u2502\n   \u2502  - JIT Compiler       \u2502                \u2502  - Garbage Collector  \u2502\n   \u2502  - Garbage Collector  \u2502                \u2502  - CTS / CLS Engine   \u2502\n   \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518                \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n              \u2502                                        \u2502\n              \u25bc Native Machine Code                    \u25bc Native Machine Code\n```\n\n### 2. Deep Dive: Legacy GUI Tools (Mandatory Historical Context for Exam)\n- **Visual Basic (VB6):** Extremely popular in the 1990s and 2000s for rapid application development (RAD). Event-driven; used DAO/ADO to connect to Microsoft Access or SQL Server. **Formally retired by Microsoft in 2008**.\n- **Oracle Developer 2000 (D2K) / Oracle Forms & Reports (iDS):**\n  - Tightly coupled with Oracle Database. Developers wrote PL/SQL triggers behind form blocks and items.\n  - Used widely in early Indian Railways computerized depots and workshop inventory systems. **Deprecated for modern web developments**.\n- **PowerBuilder:** DataWindow technology for rapid database querying without manual SQL writing. Largely replaced by web frameworks.\n\n### 3. Application Development in COBOL (Common Business-Oriented Language)\nHistorically powered Indian Railways mainframe batch applications (PRS and freight accounting):\n- **Structure of a COBOL Program:**\n  1. `IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.` (Program name, author, date written).\n  2. `ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.` (`CONFIGURATION SECTION.`, `INPUT-OUTPUT SECTION.` - file mapping via `SELECT ... ASSIGN TO ...`).\n  3. `DATA DIVISION.` (`FILE SECTION.`, `WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.` - data variables).\n  4. `PROCEDURE DIVISION.` (Executable procedural logic: `PERFORM`, `IF`, `EVALUATE`, `MOVE`, `COMPUTE`).\n- **Data Definitions & Picture Clauses:**\n  - `PIC 9(5)` -> 5-digit integer.\n  - `PIC X(20)` -> 20-character alphanumeric string.\n  - `PIC 9(4)V99` -> 6 digits with 2 decimal places (implied decimal `V`).\n  - `01 EMPLOYEE-RECORD.`\n    - `05 EMP-ID PIC 9(6).`\n    - `05 EMP-NAME PIC X(30).`\n    - `05 EMP-BASIC-PAY PIC 9(6)V99.`\n- **File Handling in COBOL:**\n  - Sequential files processed using `OPEN INPUT`, `READ ... AT END`, `WRITE`.\n  - Indexed files (VSAM) accessed randomly via `RECORD KEY` using `START`, `READ`, `REWRITE`.\n",
